### The homepage should answer five questions quickly

1. What do you build? Business websites, custom software, integrations, and automation.

2. Who do you help? Ugandan businesses and organizations, with the ability to work remotely with international clients.

3. Why choose you? Reliable delivery, locally relevant integrations, strong engineering controls, and clear ownership.

4. Can I trust you? Real work, transparent scope, demonstrable quality, and no fabricated claims.

5. How do I start? A direct WhatsApp conversation or a concise project inquiry.

A suitable positioning direction—not final approved copy—is:

> Software that solves real business problems. Engineered to work in the real world.
>
> We build fast websites, custom business systems, integrations, and workflow automation for organizations in Uganda and beyond.

The actual homepage should be written around concrete client outcomes, rather than filling the hero with a list of frameworks or generic phrases such as “innovative digital transformation.”

## 6. Design direction: premium without being heavy

The site should look like a serious engineering studio, not a generic agency template and not an overloaded enterprise consultancy homepage.

Direction A — Editorial engineering

Strong typography, deliberate whitespace, restrained color, diagrams and technical proof. Best for communicating competence and clarity.

Direction B — Technical studio

A more technical identity with diagrams, structured grids, and controlled dark surfaces. Useful if applied selectively rather than turning every page into a dark dashboard.

These are visual reference directions, not instructions to copy another firm's branding.

My recommendation is to combine the clarity of editorial design with selective technical elements. The design should use a consistent spacing scale, restrained typography, a defined color system, accessible components, and reusable responsive patterns.

Avoid:

* Large autoplay videos.

* Endless scrolling animations.

* A wall of generic stock photography.

* Artificially inflated metrics.

* Excessive gradients, glowing cards, and decorative code.

* Huge animated hero text that delays access to the actual offer.

* A separate, visually inconsistent style for every service page.

Use real product screenshots, diagrams, annotated interface examples, and carefully selected photography when they add information. Visual polish should come from hierarchy, typography, composition, and detail—not from the volume of effects.

## 7. How to convert visitors into clients

The website should support more than one buying journey.

Path A — ready to talk

A prominent WhatsApp contact option for buyers who want to explain a problem quickly. The current website already uses `https://wa.me/256760779160`; preserve it unless the owner changes it.

Path B — request a project assessment

A short form asking what the organization does, what problem it needs solved, the desired outcome, the approximate timeline, and an optional budget range. Collect only information needed to qualify the enquiry.

Path C — researching a service

Specific service pages, practical FAQs, process explanations, and real examples for visitors who are not ready to contact the company yet.

Path D — evaluating a vendor

Engineering standards, ownership and handover commitments, security approach, maintenance options, and case studies to help a buyer evaluate risk.

### Pricing and packages

Pricing deserves special attention in the Ugandan market, but publishing low prices without understanding delivery cost can make the business unsustainable.

I recommend three commercial models:

* Fixed-scope packages: appropriate for well-defined websites, audits, and small integrations.

* Scoped projects: written requirements, assumptions, deliverables, acceptance criteria, milestones, and a clear price.

* Ongoing care plans: a defined monthly scope with explicit response windows, exclusions, and escalation procedures.

Publish starting prices only after costing the work properly. If a project requires discovery, make that explicit. Do not promise a fixed price before the scope is understood, and do not imitate competitors' price points simply because they appear attractive.

For international clients, explain remote collaboration, communication, ownership, handover, and payment arrangements only to the extent the business can actually support them.

## 8. Technical, security, and search requirements

The website is a client acquisition system. It should be engineered and operated accordingly.

| Area              | Required outcome                                                                                                                                         |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Architecture      | Semantic, maintainable pages and reusable components; avoid unnecessary client-side complexity.                                                          |
| SEO               | Unique titles and descriptions, logical headings, canonical URLs, sitemap, robots policy, structured data where appropriate, and useful service content. |
| Security          | HTTPS, safe headers, dependency hygiene, no exposed secrets, input validation, spam controls, and no unnecessary collection of personal data.            |
| Forms             | Server-side validation and abuse protection if backed by a service; clear confirmation and failure behavior; no false success messages.                  |
| Analytics         | Measure service-page engagement and qualified contact actions with privacy controls and sensible retention.                                              |
| Reliability       | Monitor actual availability and errors; document deployment and rollback; distinguish synthetic tests from real user evidence.                           |
| Accessibility     | WCAG 2.2 AA target with automated checks and manual verification.                                                                                        |
| Performance       | Test real production assets, mobile viewports, throttled networks, and Core Web Vitals.                                                                  |
| Content integrity | No fake testimonials, invented client logos, fabricated project results, or unverified compliance claims.                                                |
| Maintainability   | CI checks, repeatable builds, regression tests, clear documentation, and controlled releases.                                                            |

The current site uses Cloudflare Pages and PostHog according to the existing project record. Those services should be retained if the audit confirms they are correctly configured; changing infrastructure is not a design requirement.

## 9. What the site should prove, not merely claim

A small firm can compete on engineering discipline, but only if it can demonstrate that discipline.

Build the trust system around evidence:

* Work samples: genuine projects, with permission to publish where necessary.

* Demonstrations: clearly labeled sample applications when client work is not available to show.

* Engineering standards: actual test strategy, deployment approach, accessibility process, and security boundaries.

* Delivery clarity: scope, milestones, acceptance, handover, and ownership.

* Local capability: accurate descriptions of relevant mobile-money and business-system integration experience.

* Maintenance: a concrete description of what happens after launch.

* Measured performance: current test results from the actual website, not aspirational badges.

* Transparent limitations: clearly state when a service requires a specialist partner or additional client authorization.

The company does not need fabricated enterprise logos or made-up percentages to look credible. A small number of well-explained, verifiable demonstrations will be stronger than an impressive but unsupported portfolio.

## 10. Research-derived priorities

Priority 1 — establish a focused offer

Lead with business websites, custom software, integrations and reconciliation, workflow automation, software rescue, and ongoing engineering care.

Priority 2 — build credibility into the site

Make delivery process, engineering standards, ownership, case studies, and contact options first-class parts of the experience.

Priority 3 — optimize for constrained devices

Set performance budgets, target Core Web Vitals, keep critical resources light, and test accessibility and usability on mobile.

Priority 4 — differentiate through integrations

Make payment workflows, reconciliation, EFRIS integration, and business automation discoverable, with honest descriptions of current capability and authorization requirements.

Priority 5 — keep claims and operations aligned

No unverified service promises, fabricated proof, unsupported compliance statements, or operational commitments beyond available capacity.

